TI  - Association of adiponectin levels and polymorphisms in ADIPOQ and ADIPOR2 genes with metabolic syndrome and atherogenic index of plasma
PY  - 2014
T2  - Experimental and Clinical Cardiology
VL  - 20
IS  - 8
SP  - 3692
EP  - 3705
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84906854458&partnerID=40&md5=5aeef3e5d6ea0d4c595546b2120f023c
AB  - Background and aim. Adiponectin exerts a protective effect due to its anti-inflammatory and anti-atherogenic effects. Variation in the ADIPOQ and ADIPOR2 genes have been associated with increased risk of metabolic syndrome (MetS), type-2 diabetes, or coronary heart disease (CHD). The purpose of this study was to analyze the relationship between adiponectin levels and six polymorphisms in ADIPOQ and ADIPOR2 genes with the MetS and atherogenic index of plasma (AIP). Methods. We studied 115 women with MetS and 231 without MetS. Anthropometric measurements, blood pressure, blood levels of adiponectin, glucose, cholesterol, triglycerides, HDL-c and LDL-c were determined. Genotyping was performed by real-time-PCR. Results. Adiponectin levels were higher in women without MetS (p<0.001) and were negatively correlated with age, waist circumference, blood pressure, glucose, triglycerides and AIP. Women carrying the GG genotype of rs1501299 had higher AIP (p=0.01) compared with carriers of the genotype GT/TT. Conclusion. The decrease of adiponectin may contribute to the development of MetS and atherosclerosis and may be an important marker in risk stratification of CHD and be useful for its diagnosis and prevention. The other hand variations in the ADIPOQ gene might be involved in atherogenic risk.
