TI  - Orally and transdermally replaced estradiol improves endothelial function equally in middle-aged women after surgical menopause
PY  - 2003
T2  - American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology
VL  - 188
IS  - 5
SP  - 1291
EP  - 1296
DO  - 10.1067/mob.2003.326
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0038395946&doi=10.1067%2fmob.2003.326&partnerID=40&md5=4b2f2a82b6d4fa3ee1ab44088f4f0e27
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Improvement in endothelial function may be an important mechanism by which estrogen replacement therapy protects postmenopausal women against coronary artery disease. We determined whether the vascular effects of estradiol depend on the route of administration. STUDY DESIGN: Six weeks after surgically induced menopause, 43 healthy women were assigned randomly to 28 weeks of treatment by either orally or transdermally replaced estradiol. Endothelium-dependent and endothelium-independent dilation were calculated with the use of the diameters of the brachial artery that were measured at rest by high resolution ultrasound scanning after reactive hyperemia and after sublingual glyceryl trinitrate. RESULTS: Endothelium-dependent dilation increased after oral estradiol replacement from 6% ± 3.9% to 13.2% ± 4.4% (P < .0001) and after transdermal estradiol replacement from 7% ± 4.9% to 14.9% ± 5.6% (P < .0001). Endothelium-independent dilation did not change significantly in either group. The improvements in endothelium-dependent dilation after estrogen substitution were independent of the changes in blood lipids and lipoproteins. CONCLUSION: Both oral and transdermal long-term replacement of estradiol lead to improved endothelial function in healthy middle-aged women after surgically induced menopause.; Indications:Prophylaxis of surgically induced menopause in 21 patients.; Patients:43 healthy women. Estraderm TTS group: n=21, mean age 47.8 ± 4.1 years. Oral estradiol group: n=20, mean age 49.2 ± 4.0 years.; TypeofStudy:A prospective, double-blind study comparing the effects of prolonged administration of transdermal (Estraderm TTS) or oral estradiol (E2) on endothelium-dependent and endothelium-independent vasodilation in healthy women after surgically induced menopause.; DosageDuration:50 mg daily given as a patch transdermally. Duration was 28 weeks.; ComparativeDrug:Estrogen (Estrofem) 2 mg daily given as a tablet orally. Duration was 28 weeks.; AdverseEffects:No adverse events were mentioned.; Results:1 woman from each group did not complete the study for personal reasons. No woman in the study used <92% of the estimated dosages of medication. There were no differences between the two groups at the beginning of the study in age, in active smoking, in systolic and diastolic blood pressure, in body mass index, and in waist-to-hip ratio. All women were normotensive. After the treatment period, these parameters were unchanged. There were no differences in serum lipid and lipoprotein values between the two groups at the beginning or end of the study. After oral E2 administration, LDL-C levels decreased by 13.4% (P< .01) and Lp(a) lipoprotein by 18.1% (P < .05), and HDL-C levels increased by 15.8% (P < .05). After Estraderm TTS, triglyceride levels decreased by 10.2% (P < .02), and HDL-C levels increased by 8.9% (P < .03). The total cholesterol/HDL-C ratio and LDL-C/HDL-C ratio decreased in each group, the decrease of the latter being significant only in the oral E2 group. No differences between groups were observed in the baseline diameters and increases in blood flow during reactive hyperemia of the brachial arteries. In the group in which E2 was replaced orally, the FMD increased from 6.0% ± 3.9% to 13.2% ± 4.4% (P< .001); in the Estraderm TTS group, the FMD increased from 7.0% ± 4.9% to 14.9% ± 5.6% (P < .001). The improvement in FMD of the brachial artery was not significantly different between the groups. FMD improved in all women in each groups. GTN-mediated dilation did not change significantly in either group. At the beginning and end of the study, we found no correlation of FMD with any clinical or laboratory characteristics. The improvement of FMD was independent of changes in lipid parameters.; AuthorsConclusions:In conclusion, the results of our study show that, in healthy middle-aged women after surgical menopause, oral and transdermal E2 replacement improves endothelial function equally, independently of their lipid modifying effects. Although the significance of the improvement in endothelial function for long-term prognosis of atherosclerotic disease is unknown, we can assume that it may result in the stabilization of atherosclerotic plaques and that the route of E2 replacement does not affect these beneficial effects in the process of atherosclerosis.; FreeText:Power calculations were performed with the assumption that the study should be able to detect a true difference in flow-mediated dilation (FMD) dilation of 2%, with 90% probability at the two-sided 5% level of significance. With an assumed standard deviation (SD) of FMD measurement of 3%, the minimum target trial size was estimated to be 19 subjects in each group. The power calculations were done before the study, and the power of the study was >0.9. At clinical examination, systolic and diastolic blood pressures were measured. Anthropometric parameters were determined, and body mass index was calculated. Waist circumference was measured. Blood for the analysis of lipids, and Lp(a) lipoprotein was collected. Concentrations of total cholesterol, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C), and triglycerides were determined by standard colorimetric assays. Low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) was calculated from Friedewald's formula. The atherogenic indices were calculated as the ratio between triglycerides and HDL-C and between LDL-C and HDL-C. Lp(a) lipoprotein was measured by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. The endothelium-dependent and endothelium-independent dilation of the right brachial artery were studied with a high-resolution B-mode ultrasound system. The subjects rested in the supine position for 10 minutes before hemodynamic measurements were performed. The right brachial artery was scanned in the longitudinal section 2 to 15 cm above the elbow to find the clearest images of the anterior and posterior wall layers. The mean arterial diameter was measured at the end of diastole, which was determined by the simultaneous monitoring of the electrocardiogram (ECG; concurrent with the onset of QRS complex). The blood flow velocity was measured at a fixed incident angle of 68 degrees to the vessel with the range gate (1.3 mm), which was located in the center of the artery. The baseline (resting) blood flow was estimated by multiplying the velocity time integral of the Doppler blood flow signal (corrected for incident angle) by the vessel cross-sectional area. Hyperemic blood flow increase was induced by the inflation of a blood pressure tourniquet. Hyperemic flow (with an increased blood flow that produced an endothelium-dependent stimulus to vasodilation) was recorded for the first 15 seconds; diameter measurements were taken 45 to 60 seconds after cuff deflation. A relative blood flow increase during reactive hyperemia was calculated as the maximum blood flow recorded during the first 15 seconds after cuff release, divided by the blood flow during rest. The endothelium-dependent dilation was expressed as the percentage of the change of the diameter after reactive hyperemia relative to the baseline scan. A sublingual tablet of 0.5 mg of glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) was then administered; 4.5 minutes later, the final scan was performed. The endothelium-independent dilation was expressed as the percentage of change in the diameter after GTN administration, relative to the baseline scan.
