TI  - Ezetimibe/simvastatin: A review of its use in the management of hypercholesterolemia
PY  - 2004
T2  - American Journal of Cardiovascular Drugs
VL  - 4
IS  - 6
SP  - 405
EP  - 422
DO  - 10.2165/00129784-200404060-00009
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-10644252849&doi=10.2165%2f00129784-200404060-00009&partnerID=40&md5=e6d4a104869f5e97bd7d76b48dd5d2a0
AB  - Ezetimibe/simvastatin (Inegy™, Vytorin™) therapy combines two lipid-lowering compounds with complementary mechanisms of action, thereby blocking the two sources of plasma cholesterol and improving lipid profiles. Thus, intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol and related phytosterols is blocked by ezetimibe, with the biosynthesis of cholesterol in the liver inhibited by simvastatin. Developing management trends for primary hypercholesterolemia include the aggressive reduction of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) to goals lower than previously considered appropriate, the targeting of lipid subtractions and atherogenic indices other than LDL-C alone, and the broader use of combination lipid-lowering therapy. In line with these trends, ezetimibe/simvastatin is an effective and generally well tolerated adjunct to dietary therapy for markedly reducing LDL-C levels, providing a 52% reduction with the recommended starting dosage. Furthermore, ezetimibe/simvastatin is formulated with variable doses of simvastatin (i.e. 10/10mg, 10/20mg, 10/40mg, and 10/80mg) and hence, the dosage may be adjusted to suit the individual patient's needs. As longer-term efficacy, tolerability, economic and outcome data accrue, ezetimibe/simvastatin will be positioned more definitively relative to existing and emerging lipid-lowering treatments. Currently, ezetimibe/simvastatin therapy represents a valuable novel option for the management of primary hypercholesterolemia across diverse patient populations and as an adjunct to other lipid-lowering treatments in those with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. Pharmacologic Properties. Plasma cholesterol is derived from intestinal absorption and biosynthesis. Ezetimibe/simvastatin combines two lipid-lowering agents with complementary mechanisms of action and thus inhibits both of these sources of plasma cholesterol. Ezetimibe and its primary glucuronide metabolite localize to the small intestine and selectively inhibit the absorption of cholesterol and related phytosterols. Simvastatin, an inactive prodrug, is hydrolyzed in the liver to β-hydroxysimvastatin, which inhibits HMG-CoA reductase and hepatic cholesterol synthesis. Importantly, the ezetimibe/simvastatin tablet formulation is bioequivalent to coadministered ezetimibe plus simvastatin. Ezetimibe is rapidly absorbed after oral administration, and undergoes glucuronidation in enterocytes. Both ezetimibe and its glucuronide localize in the intestinal lumen with minimal systemic distribution. The pharmacokinetics of ezetimibe are not significantly altered by concomitant food ingestion, age, bodyweight, sex, race, or renal impairment. However, ezetimibe is not recommended in patients with moderate or severe hepatic impairment. Ezetimibe has no major influence on the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, and has shown no clinically significant drug interactions with statins or fibrates. Importantly, ezetimibe does not significantly influence the pharmacokinetics of simvastatin. Simvastatin is well absorbed after oral administration and undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism. Simvastatin and its primary active metabolite, β-hydroxysimvastatin, localize in the liver, with minimal systemic distribution. The pharmacokinetics of simvastatin are largely independent of age and sex, and dosage adjustments are considered unnecessary in patients with mild or moderate renal impairment. Drug interactions may occur between simvastatin and other agents that are inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4, other compounds that may cause myopathy, and coumarin anticoagulants. Therapeutic Efficacy. In 12- to 23-week randomized, double-blind, multicenter clinical trials, ezetimibe/simvastatin combination therapy improved lipid profiles in patients with primary hypercholesterolemia, reducing atherogenic lipids (e.g. LDL-C, triglycerides, total cholesterol, non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol [non-HDL-C], and apolipoprotein B) and increased anti-atherogenic lipids (e.g. HDL-C). Pooled-dosage analyses indicated that ezetimibe/simvastatin 10/10-10/80 mg/day reduced mean plasma LDL-C levels (50-53% reduction) considerably more than ezetimibe 10 mg/day monotherapy (18-20%) or simvastatin 10-80 mg/day monotherapy (36-39%) [primary endpoint in most trials]. These differences were generally consistent across the subgroups analyzed. In individual-dosage analyses, the lowest-dosage ezetimibe/simvastatin combination (10/10 mg/day) reduced mean plasma LDL-C levels to a similar extent as the highest dosage of simvastatin monotherapy (80 mg/day). Moreover, the decrease in plasma LDL-C level with ezetimibe/simvastatin treatment was significantly greater than for the corresponding or next-higher schedule of simvastatin monotherapy. In addition, significantly more ezetimibe/simvastatin than simvastatin monotherapy recipients attained LDL-C goals (75-87% vs 43-64%) [primary endpoint in one trial]. Reductions in plasma LDL-C levels in ezetimibe/simvastatin recipients were sustained for at least 48 weeks in a 48-week double-blind extension phase of a 6-week double-blind trial. Ezetimibe/simvastatin was effective across diverse patient populations, including in hypercholesterolemic patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus and in those with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. Pharmacoeconomic Considerations. A decision analytic model showed that coadministration of ezetimibe 10 mg/day plus simvastatin 10 mg/day increased remaining life expectancy compared with statin titration in patients with coronary heart disease (CHD) who fail to reach target total cholesterol levels with simvastatin 10 mg/day alone. In a Finnish study, ezetimibe/simvastatin was projected to increase remaining life expectancy by 0.19-1.91 years compared with a 42% uptitration rate for patients not at target levels and by 0.03-0.86 years compared with a 100% uptitration rate for patients not at goal (year of costing not reported). In a Spanish cost-effectiveness analysis in CHD or CHD-equivalent patients, ezetimibe/simvastatin was shown to be a cost-effective alternative to an atorvastatin titration strategy in patients who failed to attain their LDL-C goal with atorvastatin alone. Ezetimibe/simvastatin 10/20 mg/day was projected to increase life expectancy by 0.34 years versus atorvastatin titrated to reach LDL-C goal in CHD patients and by 0.18 years in CHD-equivalent patients, with a discounted incremental cost per life-year saved with ezetimibe/simvastatin treatment of €8685 and €17 269, respectively (year of costing not reported). Tolerability. Ezetimibe/simvastatin combination therapy was generally well tolerated in patients with primary hypercholesterolemia, with an overall tolerability profile similar to those of its constituents and placebo. Treatment-related adverse events were documented in 14-20% of combination therapy recipients versus 13-19% of simvastatin-treated patients. No serious treatment-related adverse events were reported in ezetimibe/simvastatin during clinical trials. In pooled data from three similarly designed placebo-controlled trials, adverse events reported in at least 2% of ezetimibe/simvastatin recipients (10/10, 10/20, 10/40, or 10/80 mg/day) and at a greater incidence than placebo regardless of causality were headache (6.8% vs 6.4% with placebo treatment), upper respiratory tract infection (3.9% vs 2.6%), influenza (2.6% vs 1%), myalgia (3.5% vs 2.9%), and pain in extremity (2.3% vs 1.3%). In any individual trial, up to 2% of ezetimibe/simvastatin-treated patients experienced sustained elevations of plasma transaminase levels. Although in one study significantly more patients given ezetimibe/simvastatin than simvastatin had such elevations, these increases were generally asymptomatic and no patients had hepatitis or other signs of hepatotoxicity. In these trials, the overall incidence of creatine kinase elevations was ≤1%, and no ezetimibe/simvastatin recipients experienced myopathy or rhabdomyolysis. © 2004 Adis Data Information BV. All rights reserved.
