AB  - Ischemic heart disease (IHD) is a most common manifestation of generalized atherosclerosis. Hyperlipidemia is one of the most significant risk factors among over 250 factors causing atherosclerosis. Because of this, statin therapy is the "guideline" in therapy of hyperlipoproteinemia. The aim of this study was to show the hypolipemic effect of statins. The research included 74 patients with hyperlipoproteinemia type II and III, with (59 patients) or without (15) coronary disease. All patients have been treated with statins (70 by atorvastatin, 4 by simvastatin). In all patients, we analized hypolipemic effects of statins. Having in mind the pharmacological effects of statins (2, 3), the research was carried out: before therapy, after 2 and after 6 weeks, 3 months, and than every 3 months in a cumulative period of 2 years of therapy. The results of this study showed that the effect of statins on lipoprotein profile and atherogenic index UH/HDL and LDL/HDL, after 3-month therapy was significant. The pharmacological statin effects reached the maximum after 3 months-therapy, and stayed constant. Target values of lipoprotein levels were achieved after 3-6 months of statin treatment. The statin therapy have significant effect on lipoprotein profile and atherogenic index. This effect is the most intensive after 3 month therapy, and target levels of lipoproteins are achieved after 3-6 months of statin treatment.
