UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-29444437434&partnerID=40&md5=25f79b1db5fc1da1187a86bc516f83ce
AB  - Dislipidemia, persisting prethrombotic state and elevation of anticardiolipin antibodies are risk factors for coronary artery disease. This study was aimed to perform parallel investigation of serum lipids and hemostasis indexes of 39 patients with clinically proven coronary artery disease: 13 with stable angina; 13 with unstable angina and 13 after myocardial infarction. The following parameters were estimated: TC, HDL-C, LDL-C, TG, apo AI, apo B and atherogenic indices TC/HDL-C, TG/HDL-C, non-HDL-C, apo B/AI, platelet count and aggregation, platelet factor 4, coagulation factors I, II, VII, IX, X, aPTT, antithrombin III, protein C, plasminogen, D-dimer, plasma viscosity and anticardiolipin antibodies. TC, LDL-C and non - HDL were significantly higher in patients with stable angina compared to patients with unstable angina, while apo B/AI was lowered. Patients after myocardial infarction were with significantly higher apo B/AI and anticardiolipin antibodies and lower platelet count and HDL-C in comparison with patients with stable angina. TC/HDL-C, non-HDL-C and anticardiolipin antibodies were significantly higher while fibrinogen was diminished in patients with myocardial infarction compared to unstable angina. Multiple correlations between lipid and hemostasis parameters, anticardiolipin antibodies and plasma viscosity were found. Parameter changes indicated platelet activation and hypercoagulability in patients with unstable angina and after myocardial infarction.
