TI  - Coronary artery disease in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia
PY  - 1982
T2  - Respiration and Circulation
VL  - 30
IS  - 1
SP  - 87
EP  - 93
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0020046015&partnerID=40&md5=d05d5e8597de4e751d95be49ddc4a921
AB  - Coronary artery disease (CAD) is a common complication of Familial Hypercholesterolemia (FH), but some patients are not associated with CAD even if their serum cholesterol levels are high enough and they are at old age. The purpose of this study is to define the relationship between the prevalence of CAD and the lipoprotein abnormalities and Achilles tendon thickness in FH patients considering age and sex. 51 heterozygous FH patients were divided into two groups, that were patients with CAD and without CAD. The levels of total cholesterol (TC), HDL-cholesterol (HDL-C), triglyceride (TG), Atherogenic Index (AI) and Achilles tendon thickness (ATT) were detected and compared between each other. Male patients had higher frequencies of CAD than female patients and the onset of CAD was at least 10 years earlier in male patients than those of female patients in agreement with previous papers. There were only a few male patients more than 50 years old who had not yet been attached by CAD. HDL-C levels were significantly lower in patients with CAD than in those without CAD. The higher the HDL-C levels, the lower the frequencies of CAD, and under 60 years old, there were no CAD patients with HDL-C for more than 50 mg/dl. There was no significant difference in levels of TC and TG between those with CAD and those without CAD. The higher the AI, the higher the frequencies of CAD, and there was positive relationship between AI and prevalence of CAD. Achilles tendon was significantly thicker in patients with CAD than in those without CAD, and the thicker Achilles tendon the patients had, the higher frequencies of CAD they had.
